WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.650
Ask who owns an AI agent's identity
and three offices answer at once.

2
00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:09.038
The way out is to separate the decisions
and give each one a single decider.

3
00:00:09.038 --> 00:00:12.687
AI adds one new executive
and one new kind of decision.

4
00:00:12.687 --> 00:00:18.413
The Chief AI Officer owns the inventory,
the high-impact determinations and waivers.

5
00:00:18.413 --> 00:00:22.763
The official accountable for the mission
signs the use-case risk acceptance.

6
00:00:22.763 --> 00:00:25.052
Neither one authorizes the system;

7
00:00:25.052 --> 00:00:29.712
the authorizing official alone
accepts its security and privacy risk.

8
00:00:29.712 --> 00:00:33.387
For the identity itself, the
sponsor, an agency employee,

9
00:00:33.387 --> 00:00:36.024
decides whether the agent should exist.

10
00:00:36.024 --> 00:00:39.788
The technical owner, who may be
a contractor, keeps it running.

11
00:00:39.788 --> 00:00:44.663
And the identity governance officer
decides consent, transfers and orphans.

12
00:00:44.663 --> 00:00:48.963
Two signatures stay two, even
when one person holds both roles.

13
00:00:48.963 --> 00:00:51.000
Now, the inventory scan.
